Task: HDD/CDD threshold sensitivity sweep (Persistence Extensions — Phase 5)

WHY: High_CDD/High_HDD use an arbitrary p80 top-quintile cut on the 1990-2000
baseline. Does the headline cold finding survive a stricter cut, and are the
degree-day burden coefficients robust to the threshold choice?

WHAT CHANGED:
- New Code/run_threshold_sensitivity.R: re-derives High_CDD/High_HDD at p70, p80
  (primary), p90 of the national 1990-2000 baseline and re-estimates:
    * the primary STATE spec (full climate block, swapping CDD/HDD cut), and
    * the primary county SPEC 2 (PDSI + High_CDD/HDD),
  extracting CDD/HDD coefficients (+ state is_cold_shock_lag1) at each cut.
  Self-contained baselines: STATE from analysis_ready_dataset.csv (1990-2025),
  COUNTY from Data/intermediate_climate.rds (1990-2000). 252 coef rows ->
  Analysis/threshold_sensitivity_coefs.csv + faceted plots.
- Analysis/state_analysis_summary.md: new section 8.

KEY RESULTS:
- HEADLINE COLD SURVIVES p90: is_cold_shock_lag1 -> Medical_Debt_Share is stable
  and significant at all three cuts (0.0129 p=0.007 / 0.0117 p=0.012 / 0.0138
  p=0.003). It is z-temperature based, so threshold-free by construction;
  re-defining the degree-day flags does not disturb it.
- DEGREE-DAY COEFS ARE CUTOFF-FRAGILE: only 25/234 (10.7%) High_CDD/High_HDD
  coefficients significant, and NONE significant at all three cuts -- every
  significant degree-day term flips at some cutoff. Recommendation: lead the cold
  story with is_cold_shock (robust) and PDSI drought (threshold-free), and report
  High_CDD/High_HDD with an explicit threshold-sensitivity caveat.

DECISIONS: no unit tests -- Phase 5 is a pure re-estimation sweep with no new
reusable algorithm (the plan lists no test bullet); the p80 recomputation
reproducing the existing flags is the implicit validation. Debt reporting
exclusion (CO 2023) applied to debt outcomes to match run_county_analysis.R.

FILES: Code/run_threshold_sensitivity.R, Analysis/state_analysis_summary.md.
CSV/plot outputs gitignored per project convention.
